    longDescription:
      "In the fast-paced world of tech hiring, speed and precision are everything. When an open position is posted, recruitment teams are often flooded with hundreds of resumes. Sifting through PDFs, cross-referencing screening notes, and evaluating technical stacks manually is a massive bottleneck. We solved this by treating recruitment like an engineering challenge: a fully autonomous, AI-driven HR pipeline built entirely in n8n. The workflow does not just parse resumes—it reads them, evaluates them against live internal criteria, creates robust tracking tasks in our ATS (Teamwork), and delivers beautifully formatted briefings right to our inboxes.\n\nThe tech stack\n\n• n8n — The core automation engine orchestrating the entire workflow.\n• Gmail API — Ingesting inbound applications and sending outbound reports (including polite rejections and role-closed notices).\n• Groq (LLMs) — Powering our AI agents with 20b and 120b models for blazing-fast, high-accuracy text analysis.\n• Google Sheets API — Acting as our dynamic, living database for active roles and grading criteria (Organizational, Function, and Role-Specific).\n• Teamwork API — Our ATS and project management hub for tracking candidate pipelines and attaching PDFs to task cards.\n\nPhase 1: Automated intake and extraction\n\nThe pipeline kicks off the moment a candidate applies. A Gmail Trigger listens for unread emails with the subject line \"candidate for.\" Once detected, the workflow downloads the attached PDF resume. A custom JavaScript node extracts the sender's email and parses the plain text of the email body—crucial because recruiters often leave valuable Call Notes (current CTC, expected CTC, notice period) there. A dedicated PDF extraction node pulls the raw text from the resume and combines everything into a single, clean candidate profile.\n\nPhase 2: Intelligent pre-screening and validation\n\nNot every resume needs a deep, expensive AI evaluation. We use a smaller, highly efficient model (Groq 20b) as the first line of defense. This AI agent extracts core details (name, years of experience, current company, location), matches the candidate to specific open roles (e.g. WordPress FullStack Developer, NodeJS + React Developer, Project Manager), and filters out completely irrelevant applications (\"No Match Found\"). Mismatched candidates receive an instant, polite rejection email. If they match a role, the workflow queries the Google Sheet database to check if the role is still \"Active.\" If the position is filled, the candidate is automatically notified that the role is closed—no manual follow-up for HR.\n\nPhase 3: Deep AI evaluation\n\nWhen a candidate matches an active role, the workflow shifts into high gear. It pulls grading rules from three Sheets tabs: Organizational Criteria, Function Criteria, and Role-Specific Criteria—so the AI grades against our exact, real-time technical requirements. A heavy-duty LLM (Groq 120b) acts as a \"Senior Technical Recruitment Lead\" and evaluates the candidate with a strict framework: 5-star scoring (Core Skills, Experience, Tools, Domain Knowledge, AI Readiness out of 10), red-flag checks (employment gaps, page-builder dependency, lack of version control/Git), an overall Fitment Score out of 10 with detailed justification, and a screening summary that extracts CTC and location from the initial call notes.\n\nPhase 4: Seamless ATS integration\n\nOnce the AI has generated its evaluation, the data goes where hiring managers work: Teamwork. The workflow uploads the binary PDF to Teamwork's /pendingfiles.json endpoint to get a temporary file reference ID, then creates a new task card on the active hiring board. The task title includes the candidate's name, the description holds the full AI evaluation report, and the resume PDF is natively attached—no more digging through email threads.\n\nPhase 5: The executive briefing\n\nThe final step is notifying the recruitment team. Raw AI markdown is converted into a corporate-styled HTML email featuring a clean blue header with the candidate's name and applied role, a color-coded Fit Score, a structured table for Current CTC, Expected CTC, and Notice Period, the full AI evaluation with styled headers and italicized evidence, a \"View Task in Teamwork\" button, and the original PDF attached. Everything is centralized and actionable.\n\nThe business impact\n\nBy routing initial resume screening through this n8n pipeline, we changed hiring velocity: zero data entry (resumes, call notes, and task creation are hands-free), instant time-to-screen (candidates evaluated within seconds of application), and standardized baselines (every candidate graded against the same technical criteria, reducing human bias in the initial screening phase). Automation is not about replacing the human element—it is about removing administrative friction so hiring managers can focus on interviewing the best talent.",
