    id: 8,
    slug: "project-buddy",
    title: "Project Buddy: AI That Knows Your Drive, Jira & Slack",
    brand: "Enterprise AI / RAG",
    metaDescription:
      "Project Buddy: AI-powered Slack bot with Vertex AI RAG. Ask in plain language; get document-grounded answers in 1–2 seconds with source citations. Google Drive sync, multi-project corpus, 24/7.",
    shortDescription:
      "Finding answers across 47+ project docs used to take 15–30 minutes. We built an AI assistant in Slack that uses Vertex AI RAG to search your Drive (and context), return grounded answers in 1–2 seconds, and cite sources—so the whole team stays in the loop without leaving the app.",
    imageUrl: "/images/portfolio/project-buddy.png",
    tags: ["Agile", "RAG", "Slack", "Google Cloud", "Vertex AI", "Gemini", "Google Drive"],
    longDescription:
      "Project Buddy is an AI-powered enterprise knowledge assistant that lives inside Slack and answers questions about your projects by searching your own documents. We built it to cut the time spent hunting for information from 15–30 minutes down to 1–2 seconds, in a way that is reliable, cost-effective, and easy for the whole team to use. It is not a generic chatbot—it is a document-grounded Q&A system (RAG: Retrieval-Augmented Generation) that only answers from your synced content, keeping responses accurate, consistent, and traceable.\n\nThe problem we solved\n\nBefore Project Buddy, finding answers across project documentation was slow and inconsistent: 15–30 minutes per search, roughly 60% accuracy on whether people had the right or latest info, availability only during business hours, and experts constantly interrupted to answer the same questions. With 47+ documents across multiple projects (e.g. Apex Beverages, INC, Magnets, Wearwell) in Google Drive, the content was there; the problem was discovery and synthesis. We needed a single place where anyone could ask in natural language and get a fast, grounded answer with sources—without leaving Slack.\n\nHow it works (architecture)\n\nUser asks a question in Slack; Slack sends the event to our app on Cloud Run. Flask and Slack Bolt receive the request; a ThreadManager loads conversation history for follow-ups. An IntelligentRAG service queries Vertex AI RAG: the query is embedded (text-embedding-004), similar chunks are retrieved from the corpus, and Gemini generates an answer using only those chunks (plus optional history). The response is streamed back to Slack so the user sees the answer with source references in 1–2 seconds. We use RAG, not fine-tuning: we maintain a corpus of chunked, embedded documents and retrieve at query time, so updating knowledge is as simple as changing docs in Drive and resyncing. Documents are synced from Google Drive on a schedule (e.g. daily) and on-demand via a /sync command; the corpus is stored and managed in Vertex AI; the app is stateless and runs on Cloud Run.\n\nTechnology stack\n\n• Runtime: Python 3.11.\n• Web / Slack: Flask, Slack Bolt (Slack API).\n• Hosting: Google Cloud Run (serverless, 2 vCPU, 2 GB RAM, region us-east4).\n• AI / RAG: Google Vertex AI — RAG API, text-embedding-004, Gemini (e.g. 2.5 Pro / 2.0 Flash).\n• Document storage: Google Drive (read-only); main folder and project subfolders.\n• Corpus metadata: corpus_metadata.json (tracked files, sync status, corpus id).\n• Config: config.py and env vars; rag_import_config.yaml for chunk size and overlap.\n• Containers: Docker (main app image; separate sync image if needed). Gunicorn (single worker) in production.\n\nKey features\n\n• Instant answers — Typical response time 1–2 seconds (vs. 15–30 minutes for manual search).\n• Context memory — Per-thread conversation history so follow-up questions (e.g. \\\"And what about the timeline?\\\") work without repeating context.\n• Auto-sync — Documents synced from Google Drive on a schedule (e.g. daily); no manual upload for normal updates.\n• Multi-project — One bot serves multiple projects; the user does not need to pick a project first.\n• Source citations — Every answer references the documents and passages it was built from.\n• 24/7 — Available whenever Slack is, from desktop and mobile.\n• Health and admin — /status for health and corpus status; /sync to force a document sync when needed.\n\nUsage: how teams interact\n\nUsers can @mention the bot in a channel (e.g. @Project Buddy what are the requirements for Apex?), send a direct message with the same question, or use a slash command (e.g. /project-buddy what are the requirements for Apex?). No special syntax is required; users ask in plain language. Additional commands: /status to check system health (corpus ready, files tracked, sync status); /sync to trigger a document sync after adding or changing files in Drive.\n\nBusiness impact and ROI\n\nTime to find info: 15–30 min before, 1–2 sec after (~99.9% faster). Monthly time saved on the order of 160 hours (~$8,000 value at e.g. $50/hr). Accuracy improved from ~60% to ~95%; availability from business hours to 24/7. Monthly cost: Cloud Run ~$97, Vertex AI (embeddings + Gemini) ~$13, total ~$110/month. ROI is on the order of ~71× with break-even under a day, depending on usage and how you value time saved.\n\nPerformance and reliability\n\nResponse time ~1.5 s average; 95th percentile ~2.5 s; 99th ~3.5 s. Uptime target 99.95%; we have seen ~99.98% in practice. Designed for 5,000+ queries per day and 100+ concurrent users with auto-scaling (e.g. 1–10 Cloud Run instances). Cold first response was reduced from 15–30 seconds to 1–2 seconds by fully initializing the corpus before accepting traffic and using a warmup request (e.g. /warmup) so the first user does not pay for cold start.\n\nSecurity and compliance\n\nTLS 1.3 for traffic; data at rest in GCP. Slack OAuth 2.0 and bot token; GCP via service account or keys in controlled environments. Read-only access to Google Drive; no write-back. We do not store personal data; conversation history is for context only and can be scoped or retained per policy. Built on GCP (SOC 2, etc.); design is compatible with GDPR-oriented controls. Credentials and secrets are supplied via environment variables, not hardcoded.\n\nDocument sync and corpus management\n\nDocuments live in Google Drive in a main folder and project subfolders. Sync is handled by a sync service or daily job (e.g. sync_service.py, daily_sync_job.py) triggered on a schedule or by Cloud Scheduler; Drive scanning (scan_drive.py, force_sync.py) for on-demand syncs; corpus lifecycle scripts (recreate_corpus.py, update_metadata_from_corpus.py) for rebuilding or fixing the Vertex corpus. Chunking is controlled by rag_import_config.yaml (chunk size, overlap) and optional LLM-based parsing for complex docs. The app reads corpus_metadata.json to know which files are in the corpus and when they were last synced.\n\nDeployment and operations\n\nMain app is containerized (Dockerfile); a separate sync pipeline can use its own image (e.g. Dockerfile.sync). Deploy scripts (e.g. deploy.sh) build and deploy to Cloud Run; CI can use cloudbuild-sync.yaml for the sync pipeline. The app exposes /health, /readiness, and /warmup for load balancers and warmup. Logging to stdout and Cloud Logging; log level configurable. Min-instances (e.g. 1) to avoid cold starts; scaling automatic up to a defined max (e.g. 10).\n\nLessons learned\n\nRAG over fine-tuning made it easier to update knowledge and to explain answers with citations. Slack-first meant no new app to install. Blocking init plus warmup eliminated the slow first response. Thread context made follow-up questions much more useful. Possible next steps: more document types (Excel, PowerPoint), more projects, custom slash commands or filters per project, multi-language support, voice (e.g. Slack huddles), integrations (e.g. Jira/Confluence), and an analytics dashboard.",
